# Healia Frontend Design Rules

When building or modifying the Healia frontend, always follow `DESIGN.md` as the visual source of truth.

## Design

- Keep the interface simple, classy, calm, and professional.
- Prioritize clarity and usability over decoration.
- Use the colors, typography, spacing, radius, borders, and visual rules defined in `DESIGN.md`.
- Use a warm off-white background and deep teal/forest green as the primary brand direction.
- Use subtle borders and very light elevation.
- Keep layouts spacious but not empty.
- Keep content widths controlled and comfortable to read.

## Never Use

- Purple or indigo as the primary visual style.
- AI-style gradients.
- Neon effects.
- Glassmorphism.
- Heavy shadows.
- Giant glowing orbs.
- Excessive rounded cards.
- Excessive pill-shaped elements.
- Generic three-column feature-card layouts.
- Decorative AI illustrations.
- Stock medical imagery.
- Excessive animation.
- Unnecessary icons.
- Every section inside a card.
- Every heading with an icon.
- Large unnecessary hero typography.

## Components

Before creating a new component:

1. Check whether an existing component can be reused.
2. Reuse existing components when possible.
3. Keep components small and focused.
4. Do not create visually different versions of the same component without a real UX reason.
5. Keep spacing and styling consistent across screens.

## Buttons

- Use one clear primary action.
- Use descriptive action labels.
- Keep button radius moderate.
- Do not make every action a primary button.
- Do not use excessive pill-shaped buttons.

## Cards

Use cards only when they provide meaningful grouping.

Prefer:

- spacing
- typography
- borders
- subtle background differences

over creating more cards.

## Voice UI

The voice interface is a core part of Healia.

- Keep the voice interface visually calm.
- Clearly communicate Listening, Thinking, Speaking, Ready, and Error states.
- Use subtle state transitions.
- Never create a giant glowing AI orb.
- Do not use distracting animations.
- Always provide understandable text describing the current state.

## Medical Information

Medical warnings and important information must have strong visual hierarchy.

- Do not hide important warnings.
- Do not communicate urgency through color alone.
- Keep medical text readable.
- Use plain language.
- Keep disclaimers visible but secondary.

## Responsive Design

Build mobile-first.

Ensure:

- No horizontal scrolling.
- Comfortable touch targets.
- Readable text.
- Simple mobile navigation.
- Voice controls are easy to access.
- Results remain easy to scan.

Desktop layouts should not simply stretch the mobile layout.

## Accessibility

Always:

- Use semantic HTML.
- Provide accessible labels.
- Support keyboard navigation.
- Provide visible focus states.
- Maintain sufficient contrast.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Never rely only on color to communicate meaning.

## Implementation Rule

Do not introduce a new visual pattern unless it is necessary.

If a design decision is not explicitly defined in the codebase, choose the simplest option that matches the existing Healia visual language.

Before considering a frontend task complete, check the implementation against `DESIGN.md`.
